  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Confidence for impacts from the coastal storm \ntonight into Friday has significantly decreased. However, \nuncertainty still exists.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nModels are in better agreement regarding a more southerly track\nfor the coastal low, which will diminish potential impacts\nacross the forecast area. Low pressure will move from the \nMid-Atlantic region toward southern New England this evening. \nThe low will track south of the Gulf of Maine tonight, with high\npressure centered northwest of Maine. The northern edge of the \nprecipitation shield will reach Downeast areas tonight, though \namounts are still uncertain. Precipitation should start in the \nform of a rain/snow mix, then generally transition to mostly \nlight snow overnight. However, a rain/snow mix could persist \nalong the immediate Downeast coast. Snow accumulations across \nDowneast areas are generally expected to be around a half inch \nor less overnight into the AM. Not expecting much impacts as\nroad surface temperatures are warm so any accumulations will be\non grassy colder surfaces. The system exits southeast of Nova \nScotia Friday, though how rapidly precipitation diminishes is \nuncertain. Additional minor snow accumulations are possible \nearly Friday before precipitation diminishes and transitions \nback to rain. Additional uncertainty exists with very dry\nboundary layer which means precipitation tonight may take awhile\nto reach the ground with hours of Virga likely. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...There is the potential for snow showers across \nthe North Saturday, with accumulation possible across higher \nelevations.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA closed upper level low sinks south into Northern Maine\non Saturday. This will bring isolated to scattered snow showers\nacross the North. There is a chance for accumulating snow \nacross the higher elevations, most likely only a few tenths of \nan inch, however a few stronger snow showers producing a quick \ninch or two of snow at elevations above 1000 ft cannot be \ncompletely ruled out. If this occurs, travel could be locally \nhazardous there especially in locations like Route 11 north of\nAshland.",
